\section{Escaping the Bottleneck}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Motivation.}
\looseness=-1 No training-free self-extraction method achieves independence from the model's distributional prior (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). We test whether a structurally independent verifier, sharing neither architecture nor training data with the generator, provides corrective signal that escapes the same-model ceiling.

\paragraph{Method.}
\looseness=-1 We use DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, to classify (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\paragraph{Zero-shot results.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO and 55.00\% on ProofWriter. Combo $w{=}3$ yields $+2.94$\,pp on FOLIO (n.s.) and $+6.67$\,pp on ProofWriter for Mistral-7B ($p < 0.001$, significant after BH correction, $m{=}12$); all generators with SC $\geq 72\%$ show no significant improvement ($\Delta \leq +0.83$\,pp, $p > 0.4$). Same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution. Preliminary evidence suggests that zero-shot NLI escape requires the generator's SC to fall below the verifier's standalone accuracy.

\paragraph{Capability gap.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 47-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): only Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains significantly ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$); all three generators above the verifier (SC $\geq 72\%$) show no significant improvement ($\Delta \leq +0.83$\,pp). The pattern is consistent with a capability-gap model: combo gain decreases monotonically with generator SC, with breakeven near the verifier's standalone accuracy. The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures and six verifier-generator pairs (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$, $p = 0.003$, $n{=}6$; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}; Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}). Per-label analysis confirms complementary error patterns that diminish as the generator improves (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}).

